<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: ra--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10945500</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2374662</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6691258</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/bjoc.2000.1258</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Decreased DNA-PK activity in human cancer cells exhibiting hypersensitivity to low-dose irradiation</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vaganay-Ju&#x000e9;ry</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Muller</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Marangoni</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Abdulkarim</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Deutsch</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lambin</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Calsou</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Eschwege</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Salles</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Joiner</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bourhis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Unit&#x000e9; Propre de l&#x02019;Enseignement Sup&#x000e9;rieur &#x02018;Radiosensibilit&#x000e9;-Radiocarcinogen&#x000e8;se humaine&#x02019; (UPRES EA 27&#x02013;10 Pr. Eschwege), Institut Gustave Roussy, IFR No 54, Villejuif, 94805, France</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>Institut de Pharmacologie et de Biologie Structurale CNRS, Toulouse, 31077, France</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>Gray Laboratory Cancer Research Trust Mount Vernon Hospital, Northwood, Midlessex, UK</aff><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>24</day><month>07</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>07</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>83</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>514</fpage><lpage>518</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>11</month><year>1999</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><month>03</month><year>2000</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><month>04</month><year>2000</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2000, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Low-dose hyper-radiosensitivity (HRS) (below 0.5 Gy) has been extensively documented in the past few years. The molecular basis of this phenomenon remains largely unknown and the purpose of this study was to investigate the possible implication of the DNA repair DNA-PK complex. The activity of the DNA-PK complex, i.e. Ku DNA-end binding activity and kinase activity of the whole complex, was studied in 10 human cancer cell lines, 2 h after 0.2, 0.5 and 1 Gy irradiation. After low-dose irradiation (0.2 Gy), a marked decrease in DNA-PK activity was found in all six cell lines exhibiting HRS, whereas the DNA-PK activity was increased in the four cell lines which did not exhibit HRS. This modulation of DNA-PK activity was a rapid phenomenon occurring within the 2 h following low-dose radiation exposure. These data strongly suggest the implication of the DNA-PK repair complex in the HRS phenomenon. &#x000a9; 2000 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>ionizing radiation</kwd><kwd>DNA repair</kwd><kwd>low-dose hyper-radiosensitivity</kwd><kwd>DNA-PK</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>